## DESCRIPTION
## Nonlinear systems
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Calculus - multivariable')
## DBchapter('Differentiation of multivariable functions')
## DBsection('Nonlinear systems')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "parserPopUp.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

$mc_yes = PopUp(["?", "Yes", "No"], "Yes");
$mc_no = PopUp(["?", "Yes", "No"], "No");

BEGIN_PGML


Consider the linear equation [` Ax = 0 `] for [` x \in \mathbb{R}^5 `], where

>>[` A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \end{bmatrix} \quad \text{so} \qquad \mathrm{rref}(A) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \end{bmatrix} `].<<

Almost no calculations are needed for any part below.

1. Does the linear system [` Ax=0 `] globally define [` (x_1,x_2,x_3) `] as a [` C^1 `] function of [` (x_4,x_5) `]? [_]{$mc_no}

2. Does the linear system [` Ax=0 `] globally define [` (x_1,x_2,x_4) `] as a [` C^1 `] function of [` (x_3,x_5) `]? [_]{$mc_yes}

3. Does the linear system [` Ax=0 `] globally define [` (x_2,x_3,x_4) `] as a [` C^1 `] function of [` (x_1,x_5) `]? [_]{$mc_yes}
END_PGML

$showHint = 1;
BEGIN_HINT
Try to do this by looking at submatrices of \( A \) or its reduced row echelon form.
END_HINT

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

ENDDOCUMENT();